WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.630 --> 00:00:06.310
This is a Wendover Productions Video made
possible by Squarespace--Make your next move

00:00:06.310 --> 00:00:11.031
with a beautiful website from Squarespace.
This video is a bit of a continuation to my

00:00:11.031 --> 00:00:16.269
last one--Why Planes Don’t Fly Faster. I
ended up talking a lot about the Concorde--the

00:00:16.269 --> 00:00:21.269
supersonic plane--but part of the story was
left untold. Even though the Concorde failed,

00:00:21.269 --> 00:00:27.229
even though it wasn’t commercially viable,
it still had a profound effect on how we fly

00:00:27.229 --> 00:00:32.029
today. Let me explain...
Economy class is not how airlines make money.

00:00:32.029 --> 00:00:36.970
The real money, at least for the traditional
airlines, is in premium cabins. Let’s take,

00:00:36.970 --> 00:00:42.880
for example, this British Airways 777. There
are 224 total seats on this plane and it flies

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:48.020
daily between London Heathrow and Washington
Dulles airport. A roundtrip economy class

00:00:48.020 --> 00:00:53.980
ticket leaving March 15th and returning on
March 22nd costs, at the time of writing,

00:00:53.980 --> 00:01:00.360
$876. That means that, if each one of the
122 economy class seats is filled, the entire

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:08.880
back section of the plane will make the airline,
round-trip, $106,872. Meanwhile, the going

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:15.710
rate for one of their premium-economy seats
is $2,633 round-trip meaning the entire cabin

00:01:15.710 --> 00:01:23.020
will make $105,320. Already you can see that
the entire 40 seat premium-economy section

00:01:23.020 --> 00:01:27.840
makes British Airways roughly as much as the
entire economy-class section. Going up to

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:38.040
Business Class, there are 48 seats sold each
for $6,723 making the airline $322,704. The

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:48.530
14 first class seats are sold for $8,715 each
or $122,010 total. So, once again, the 14

00:01:48.530 --> 00:01:54.049
passengers at the front of the plane make
the airline more money than the 122 at the

00:01:54.049 --> 00:01:59.859
back of the plane. In total, the three premium
cabins--premium economy, business, and first--make

00:01:59.859 --> 00:02:08.009
the airline on this flight $550,034. That
means that 45% of the passengers account for

00:02:08.009 --> 00:02:13.190
84% of the airline’s revenue!
Now, I need to add some caveats. There is

00:02:13.190 --> 00:02:19.430
no airline on earth that makes half a million
dollars for a six hour flight over the Atlantic.

00:02:19.430 --> 00:02:23.690
If they did, they would be swimming in money.
The fares for this particular routing are

00:02:23.690 --> 00:02:28.890
significantly higher than the average fare
paid for that flight because they’re non-stop

00:02:28.890 --> 00:02:34.050
fares between two high-income, high-demand
cities. Of course, a majority of the passengers

00:02:34.050 --> 00:02:38.900
on that flight will not be traveling between
London and Washington, they’ll have connected.

00:02:38.900 --> 00:02:44.280
If you originate the ticket in, say, Stockholm,
and connect onto that 777 flight to Washington

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:52.210
the economy class price drops to $392, the
premium economy to $1,150, the business class

00:02:52.210 --> 00:03:00.040
to $3,025, and the first class to $5,564.
But the proportions are still roughly the

00:03:00.040 --> 00:03:05.310
same. A vast majority of the revenue comes
from a minority of passengers. This particular

00:03:05.310 --> 00:03:10.370
British Airways 777 is also a very premium-heavy
configuration because British Airways is an

00:03:10.370 --> 00:03:16.900
airline that focuses a lot on premium travel,
but still, on average, 2/3 of any airlines

00:03:16.900 --> 00:03:22.630
revenue comes from passengers in First, Business,
or Premium Economy class but this wasn’t

00:03:22.630 --> 00:03:25.880
always the case.
In the beginning of commercial aviation, there

00:03:25.880 --> 00:03:30.520
weren’t really any classes because everything
was premium. That’s not to say that planes

00:03:30.520 --> 00:03:35.380
were very luxurious--a 1920s plane looked
like this--but flying was just so expensive

00:03:35.380 --> 00:03:39.710
that the experience of flying was the luxury
itself. It’s kind of like how you don’t

00:03:39.710 --> 00:03:44.360
see Virgin Galactic selling first, business,
and economy class seats on their planned tourist

00:03:44.360 --> 00:03:49.800
flights to space. The experience itself is
the luxury. Once commercial space travel becomes

00:03:49.800 --> 00:03:53.990
commonplace, we’ll almost certainly see
a classification of the experience but until

00:03:53.990 --> 00:03:58.421
a transport method is at a cost where it’s
attainable to the normal person, it’s all

00:03:58.421 --> 00:04:02.360
first class.
In 1950 a round-trip coach fare between New

00:04:02.360 --> 00:04:10.540
York and London was $675--adjusted for inflation,
thats $6,800 today--roughly the same price

00:04:10.540 --> 00:04:15.819
as a first-class ticket on the same route
nowadays. It’s the exact same type of passenger

00:04:15.819 --> 00:04:21.150
flying in both these seats. What’s changed
is who’s flying further back in the plane.

00:04:21.150 --> 00:04:25.710
So the story of the development of airline
classes really isn’t the story of how airlines

00:04:25.710 --> 00:04:31.290
developed more and more luxurious seats, it’s
how they cut costs to allow more and more

00:04:31.290 --> 00:04:36.840
people to fly. It’s also a fascinating demonstration
of economics. Airlines have figured out a

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:42.080
way to sell the same product for different
prices to different people. The overall product

00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:46.000
that airlines are selling is the same no matter
which class you’re flying--a flight from

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:50.190
point a to point b. What’s different is
the experience within the plane.

00:04:50.190 --> 00:04:55.100
The first classification of air travel happened
in the 40s and 50s. A significant amount of

00:04:55.100 --> 00:04:59.290
revenue for airlines at the time came from
contracts for air-mail routes with the US

00:04:59.290 --> 00:05:04.251
Postal Service. These flights flew with many
stops often overnight or at odd hours. While

00:05:04.251 --> 00:05:09.040
the planes mostly carried mail, they still
had a passenger section. The first class fares

00:05:09.040 --> 00:05:13.630
got you, for example, on a non-stop flight
between New York and Chicago while the coach

00:05:13.630 --> 00:05:18.350
fare might get you on a mail flight that left
at 2am and stopped in Pittsburgh and Cleveland

00:05:18.350 --> 00:05:22.540
on the way to Chicago. While the fare was
cheaper and the flight took longer, the experience

00:05:22.540 --> 00:05:28.010
onboard the plane was largely the same.
It wasn’t until 1952 that airlines started

00:05:28.010 --> 00:05:32.940
selling the same flights for different prices.
One airline, for example, sold standard class

00:05:32.940 --> 00:05:39.680
one-way tickets between New York and London
for $395 and tourist-class tickets for $270.

00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:44.770
It was the exact same flight on the exact
same plane--the difference was in the ticket.

00:05:44.770 --> 00:05:49.820
Tourist class tickets had to be purchased
in advance and had no flexibility--you had

00:05:49.820 --> 00:05:54.121
to fly on the exact flight the ticket was
purchased for. As the name suggested, these

00:05:54.121 --> 00:05:59.550
tickets were primarily for tourists. Tourists
plan trips far in advance and don’t really

00:05:59.550 --> 00:06:04.840
need flexibility so it was no problem for
them to commit to one flight. The full-fare

00:06:04.840 --> 00:06:10.050
tickets were for the other type of traveller--the
business-person. Business travelers, first

00:06:10.050 --> 00:06:14.590
off, don’t typically pay for their own tickets.
They’re paid for by their employer so they

00:06:14.590 --> 00:06:19.440
individually don’t really care what the
ticket costs. Business travelers also require

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:24.430
flexibility and generally don’t purchase
tickets until the last minute. At the time,

00:06:24.430 --> 00:06:28.310
it was common practice to just walk up to
the counter an hour before a flight and buy

00:06:28.310 --> 00:06:33.370
a ticket. That’s what the full-fare tickets
were for. Through this system, the airlines

00:06:33.370 --> 00:06:38.520
segmented the market into two categories based
on what people were willing to pay. Over the

00:06:38.520 --> 00:06:43.320
coming decades, this was the only large classification
system in air travel.

00:06:43.320 --> 00:06:51.940
Then, between 1969 and 1978 three things happened--the
747 flew for the first time, the Concorde

00:06:51.940 --> 00:06:57.960
flew for the first time and airlines were
deregulated in the US. The 747 gave airlines

00:06:57.960 --> 00:07:03.560
the space to experiment with luxury, the Concorde
gave them the reason to, and deregulation

00:07:03.560 --> 00:07:08.380
gave them the ability to. Previously, all
airfares were heavily regulated in the US

00:07:08.380 --> 00:07:12.800
and it was difficult for airlines to charge
the cost they wanted for different classes

00:07:12.800 --> 00:07:17.901
but with deregulation airlines now had full
control over their ticket prices. Now, at

00:07:17.901 --> 00:07:21.960
the time, much of the difference was still
in the ticket. Some airlines had introduced

00:07:21.960 --> 00:07:26.300
first class fares with nicer seats, but airlines
realized that they had to start treating the

00:07:26.300 --> 00:07:30.680
business-people who bought a full-fare coach
ticket differently than the tourists paying

00:07:30.680 --> 00:07:34.990
a discounted fare. More and more of those
business travelers were just paying the tourist

00:07:34.990 --> 00:07:40.180
class fare. It began by just physically separating
the passengers. The full-fare passengers would

00:07:40.180 --> 00:07:44.820
be seated up front while the discounted fare
passengers would be put in the back. Then,

00:07:44.820 --> 00:07:50.020
some airlines started blocking out the middle
seat next to the full-fare passengers. Finally,

00:07:50.020 --> 00:07:54.290
some airlines started to build cabins with
slightly nicer seats and better amenities.

00:07:54.290 --> 00:08:00.370
But, with exceptions, airlines avoided first
class. Most focused on capturing that middle

00:08:00.370 --> 00:08:04.520
tier of traveller because the Concorde was
going to be the first class plane for the

00:08:04.520 --> 00:08:09.520
rich and famous--regular planes would be the
business and economy class... at least that’s

00:08:09.520 --> 00:08:13.300
what they thought.
Of course, as you heard about in my last video,

00:08:13.300 --> 00:08:19.040
the Concorde failed... spectacularly. Airlines
avoided first class in the 70s and 80s because

00:08:19.040 --> 00:08:24.050
of the Concorde, but as they started to catch
on to the failure of supersonic flight, select

00:08:24.050 --> 00:08:29.800
airlines slowly reintroduced first class to
subsonic planes. But the effect is still seen

00:08:29.800 --> 00:08:35.830
today. Of the dozens of airlines flying transatlantic,
only six have a first class cabin. Back in

00:08:35.830 --> 00:08:41.599
the 60s and 70s the imminent perceived competition
of the Concorde really invigorated airlines

00:08:41.599 --> 00:08:46.980
to optimize that middle class--business class--and
we likely would not have seen it as early

00:08:46.980 --> 00:08:49.880
as we did without that looming disruption
to the industry.

00:08:49.880 --> 00:08:55.231
But there’s another trend to explain--first
class is going away... again. Let’s take

00:08:55.231 --> 00:09:00.330
a look at the seat-map of a Etihad a380. Each
economy class seat on this plane takes up

00:09:00.330 --> 00:09:05.360
3.77 square feet (0.35 m²) of floorspace,
the business class seats take up 10.14 ft²

00:09:05.360 --> 00:09:10.500
(0.94 m²) of floorspace, and the first class
seats take up 35 ft² of floorspace (3.25

00:09:10.500 --> 00:09:17.320
m².) On a flight from Abu Dhabi to New York,
economy class tickets are $1,253 round-trip,

00:09:17.320 --> 00:09:24.750
business class tickets are $6,140, and first
class tickets are $14,128. That means that

00:09:24.750 --> 00:09:32.080
economy class seats make $332 per square foot,
business class seats $605 per square foot,

00:09:32.080 --> 00:09:37.800
and first class seats $403 per square foot.
The difference between economy class and business

00:09:37.800 --> 00:09:42.710
class is huge--it’s a cramped seat versus
a bed--but the difference between business

00:09:42.710 --> 00:09:47.930
class and first class is just a bit more room
and some better food. It’s very hard for

00:09:47.930 --> 00:09:52.040
airlines to sell first class for much more
than business class since the experience is

00:09:52.040 --> 00:09:57.560
largely the same but the cost for the airlines
to run a first class cabin is significantly

00:09:57.560 --> 00:10:01.950
more. Therefore, more and more airlines are
taking out their first class to just put in

00:10:01.950 --> 00:10:06.310
more business class, it just makes more money.
If an airline could fill an plane full of

00:10:06.310 --> 00:10:11.540
business-class passengers it would--its been
tried--but pretty much no route has the premium

00:10:11.540 --> 00:10:17.370
demand to fill a plane-full of business class.
Everyone in economy, in the end, is just there

00:10:17.370 --> 00:10:22.730
to fill the plane.
